  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.3. Increased capacity of member States to integrate social protection in comprehensive policy responses to support and protect workers and employers during their life and work transitions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social protection programme budget reviewed by Government, in consultations with constituents, identifying priority schemes that require additional investments.
 Coordination of delivery of social protection and employment services was mapped and identified that institutional set up in Tajikistan is the foundation for joined up service delivery to support improved labour market access of the most disadvantaged groups. The Ministry of Health and Social Protection and the Ministry of Labour, Migration and Employment progressed towards redesign of key social protection schemes. In order to support implementation of the policy, different methodological guidance and trainings were provided for staff in social protection and employment departments, enabling them to tailor response to job seekers with multiple social protection needs. Social protection budget review was prepared, emphasising the need to reallocate the budget, and to invest differently and more into core programmes. ILO&#x2019; s technical assistance also included: i) three webinars on social security standards (one for all constituents and two for FITUT; ii) assessment of the level of coordination between social protection and employment departments on the local level; iii) review of national social protection budget and institutional arrangements. A rapid survey about the situation of informal workers provided information on design options for the extension of coverage.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">8.3.1. Number of member States with new or revised integrated policy responses including social protection to support and protect workers and employers during their life and work transitions</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Federation of Independent Trade Unions of Tajikistan (FITUT) with the ILO support, has drawn up its strategy and action plan on promotion of formalization in agriculture sector. This strategy and action were informed by internal FITUT data collection an analysis. The analysis of the impact of Covid-19 pandemic on the sector carried out with the ILO technical support (February-April 2021) and the resulting recommendations for action were discussed and validated by the FITUT Executive Council in October 2021. The strategy and action plan were presented and discussed with the government representatives (ministry of labour and agriculture) as well as the representatives of the agriculture sector employers&#x2019; organizations. The strategy envisages close engagement with the state bodies in the sector and the sectoral associations of employers. The Action plan covers a period of 2021-2023.
To inform the strategy, the policy position of FITUT on introducing health insurance system &#x2014; used as a campaign material to support formalization &#x2014; has been developed to push the Government to re-start its long-delayed reform. It contains provisions to extend coverage to workers in the informal economy and provided arguments for the discussions of the topic in the Tripartite Commission (March 2021). 
The trade union Strategy on formalization in the agriculture sector was presented and discussed in May 5, 2021; and the Policy position on Healthcare reform on April 24, 2021. The ILO initiated a rapid assessment of the situation of workers in informal economy, identifying gaps in accessing social protection schemes (survey tool and methodology development, conducting survey, data analysis and data report).  The ILO provided technical and expert support to FITUT in defining a strategy and action plan towards facilitating formalization through extending its services to informal workers, dependent self-employed and those in non-standard forms of employment with a focus on agriculture sector in Feb-Apr 2021. 
The ILO provided expert support to the FITUT and its Healthcare sector affiliate on developing a trade union policy position on introduction of the healthcare insurance system that would be inclusive of all workers including informal workers and workers in non-standard forms of employment. The initiative has been submitted to the national Tripartite Commission for consideration.
The ILO supported organization of two sub-regional knowledge-sharing workshops in the framework of the SSTC project on integrated strategies for the transition from the informal to the formal economy (1 June 2021) and the implementing tools and policy approaches for the transition from the informal to the formal economy (8 June 2021) delivered by the ILO Turin Center for Tajikistan, Kyrgyzstan, Azerbaijan, Uzbekistan trade unions. 
ACTRAV guide on organizing informal economy workers and ACTRAV report &#x201c;Trade Unions in the Balance&#x201d; translated into Russian/Tajik languages, that helped FITUT in developing its strategy on promoting formalization in agriculture sector.
Capacity-building for constituents on monitoring the implementation of the State programme on reducing informality (webinars conducted in July and Dec 2021). Seven webinars followed in Nov-Dec 2021, for knowledge-sharing on an integrated approach towards formalization in the CIS: A BRICS-CIS dialogue.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">7.4.2. Number of countries with a validated diagnosis of the informal economy and/or policies, regulations or compliance mechanisms to support the transition to formality of informal workers in formal enterprises or in households</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
